.TH SET_AT 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_at \- Retrieve an element from a set by index

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB char*
.IB set_at(const set_t* set, int64 pos);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_at\fR function retrieves an element from a set by index. It takes a set and an index, and returns a pointer to the element at the specified index. If the index is out of bounds, the function returns \fBNULL\fR.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set from which to retrieve the element.
.TP
.IB pos
The index of the element to retrieve.

.SH RETURN VALUE
A pointer to the element at the specified index, or \fBNULL\fR if the index is out of bounds.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_at\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    char* element;

    set_init(&set);
    set_insert(&set, "apple");
    set_insert(&set, "banana");
    set_insert(&set, "cherry");

    element = set_at(&set, 1);
    if (element != NULL) {
        printf("Element at index 1: %s\n", element);
    } else {
        printf("Index out of bounds.\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef